<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=electric-generator">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Electric generator</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An electric generator is a machine that converts mechanical energy into electrical energy; the first electromagnetic generator, the Faraday disk, was built by the British scientist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a> in 1831, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> disc turning between the poles of a horseshoe magnet. Dynamos, which produce direct current through a commutator, followed from 1832, and the Woolrich generator of 1844 was the earliest used in industry, for electroplating. Self-excited dynamos, invented independently by Wheatstone, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siemens">Siemens</a> and Varley in 1866–1867, made high power generation possible, and generators now produce nearly all grid electricity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/electric-generator.jpg" alt="Electric generator" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siemens_Brothers_-_DC_Generator_-_Electricity_Gallery_-_BITM_-_Kolkata_2015-05-09_6509.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1831</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Britain</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The self-excited dynamo was invented independently by Wheatstone, Siemens and Varley in 1866–1867.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/electromagnetic-induction">Electromagnetic induction<span>1831</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=electric-generator">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
